1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,207
You built an app.

2
00:00:01,207 --> 00:00:02,467
Your friends love it.

3
00:00:02,467 --> 00:00:04,467
Does that mean customers will pay?

4
00:00:04,467 --> 00:00:07,978
Not yet - you have evidence of interest,
but not of paying demand.

5
00:00:08,350 --> 00:00:12,148
A business provides something customers
value in exchange for payment.

6
00:00:12,148 --> 00:00:17,735
To understand how it works, start with
three parts: customer, offer, and delivery.

7
00:00:18,154 --> 00:00:21,180
The customer is the person or organization
buying.

8
00:00:21,180 --> 00:00:23,234
Ask: who are they, and what do they need?

9
00:00:23,598 --> 00:00:28,428
The offer is what they can buy: a clear
product or service, its price, and what is included.

10
00:00:28,802 --> 00:00:31,567
Delivery is how they receive what you
promised.

11
00:00:31,567 --> 00:00:35,567
Payment can happen before or after
delivery, depending on the arrangement.

12
00:00:35,986 --> 00:00:40,632
For example, Maya sells a
seventy-nine-dollar invoicing guide to freelance designers.

13
00:00:40,938 --> 00:00:45,062
Her customers are freelance designers who
need help organizing invoices.

14
00:00:45,062 --> 00:00:47,877
Her offer is the guide for seventy-nine
dollars.

15
00:00:47,877 --> 00:00:50,242
She delivers it through a download link.

16
00:00:50,678 --> 00:00:54,085
Now compare that with an app that friends
praise but nobody buys.

17
00:00:54,085 --> 00:00:55,813
The feedback may be useful.

18
00:00:55,813 --> 00:00:58,374
It does not yet show that customers will
pay.

19
00:00:58,374 --> 00:01:03,900
A purchase gives stronger evidence of
paying demand, although one sale does not prove long-term success.

20
00:01:04,278 --> 00:01:10,065
When you examine an idea, ask: who is the
customer, what is the offer, and how will it be delivered?

21
00:01:10,422 --> 00:01:14,915
In the quiz, you'll identify these three
parts and spot what is missing in a new example.
